{
  "id": 3,
  "slug": "the-rain-store",
  "title": "The Rain Store",
  "concept": "Storing data in S3, and giving an application permission to use it with an IAM role.",
  "guidance": "full",
  "region": "us-east-1",
  "azs": 2,
  "narration": [
    {
      "id": "welcome",
      "text": "The Cloudlings want to upload photos. The application needs somewhere to keep them. A NAT Gateway is already in place so the EC2 instances can reach out of the VPC; it is locked for now, and we will question that path in Level 4.",
      "highlight": "resource:nat",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "place-bucket",
      "text": "Drag the S3 bucket to the right of the sky, outside the VPC, where it becomes the S3 cloud: S3 stands outside your VPC and spans every altitude. When that tall strip of sky lights up, let go.",
      "highlight": "palette:photos",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "resource-exists",
          "resourceId": "photos"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "first-run",
      "text": "Fly the kites and watch the uploads. After a kite is delivered, the EC2 instance sends the photo on to the bucket as rain.",
      "highlight": "button:fly",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "explain",
      "text": "The rain evaporated at the bucket. The EC2 instances have no IAM role, so they have no credentials, and IAM denies everything that is not explicitly allowed. Notice the red kite too: Block Public Access shut it out.",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "place-role",
      "text": "Drag the IAM role up into the band marked High – account, above the VPC. Roles belong to your AWS account, not to any one network. When the band lights up, let go.",
      "highlight": "palette:app-role",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "resource-exists",
          "resourceId": "app-role"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "allow-write",
      "text": "Click the role and add a statement: Allow s3:Write on photo-bucket. Just that: the application only uploads.",
      "highlight": "resource:app-role",
      "pulse": ["role-statements:app-role"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "role-allows",
          "roleId": "app-role",
          "action": "s3:Write",
          "resourceId": "photos"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "attach",
      "text": "Now give the role to both EC2 instances: click each EC2 instance and choose the role (or drag from the role's handle to each EC2 instance). A fine thread will join them.",
      "highlight": "resource:app-a",
      "pulse": ["instance-role:app-a", "instance-role:app-b", "role-wearers:app-role"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "role-attached",
          "instanceIds": ["app-a", "app-b"]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "second-run",
      "text": "Fly the kites again.",
      "highlight": "button:fly",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "level-complete"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wrap",
      "text": "The uploads land. No passwords or keys were stored anywhere: the EC2 instances borrow their permissions from the role. And the bucket stayed private the whole time.",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    }
  ],
